[Music]  hey everybody  a few of us who worked on this film  called soul wanted to  tell you a little bit about one of our  favorite scenes i'm pete doctor the  director  hi i'm dana murray the producer hey i'm  mike jones i'm one of the co-writers and  i'm ken powers the co-director at  co-writer  and uh the scene we want to talk about  is uh really the anchor of the whole  movie  for many of us we have these dreams and  aspirations in our lives and  deep down we think once we've achieved  these dreams our lives are somehow going  to be  complete all wrapped up in a nice bow  but more often  we find that hey we're still the same  and as great as this event was  somehow it didn't fix everything and  we're left wondering  well did i do something wrong what's  what's the right way what's life really  all about  so here after a lifetime of trying our  main character joe  finally has a chance to play with  legendary saxophonist dorothea williams  and  it is the night of his life  i think what a lot of people might not  realize is that obsession musicians  actually matched up to dorothea williams  band so your saxophone player  was a black woman the bass player was an  asian woman there was a brother  a young brother on the drums yeah and of  course john batiste on piano  but that was like a really cool touch to  be in that musical session and seeing  the  the people playing the live music  reflecting the characters on  stage it makes me  really want to go to a show i miss shows  it goes back to the inspirations for the  whole thing  and we were really struggling with ways  to express  her like we were thinking oh yeah  dorothea has been here before too  she's been through this and she's moved  past it and so  what advice is she going to give to him  in a sort of miles davis  enigmatic way yeah the butter notes yeah  herbie hancock told us at one point he  was playing and he was struggling  and miles said don't play the butter  notes  and he's like what what what does that  even mean  he wouldn't tell you what it meant it  was just up to you to figure it out  so we had one version where she just  said you know what  i had the best cup of coffee today  and walked away and it felt kind of  dismissive like she wasn't actually  listening to him  says the older fish that's what she in  right now  this says young fish this is water  what i want is the ocean that felt just  enough like okay i heard what you're  saying  and i'm answering you but you're gonna  have to think about this  dorothea totally knows what she's doing  yeah  i love that transition too i feel like  a lot of times when you're in your own  head and you're thinking about something  the world changes and you don't you just  stay  put and so that idea of the cross  dissolve matching to the subway  was meant to feel that that was largely  inspired by  trent and atticus trent specifically  mentioned that he really connected  with joe's story this idea that somehow  this accomplishment is going to fix  things it's going to make everything  make sense and put it in order and it  doesn't  and he had a very personal story of  having gone through that himself which  it actually really helped steer us  through the storytelling it was because  of that  that we put that scene with the subway  [Music]  in  we were looking for some way to express  the idea that joe  would discover in this moment that  the little things in his life that he  would have dismissed  or walked right past those are  important those are sacred we had this  idea that  he could remove the sheet music which  sort of represents the plan that joe  thought he had for his life and put  those objects down  in its place i had written  this near the uh death of my father  he had just passed away my father had  slowly  uh kind of faded away through dementia  and it was  memory and the retaining of those  memories was always really crucial to me  and  in our last interactions i brought i'd  bring a lot of photos to him and a lot  of items and  you would find those sparks and you  would see the  the life kind of come back in his eyes a  bit i i think it was really just a quick  step from that  into this movie i mean we're always  required to  bring our heart into this and man that  was right up front for me at that time  so we thought about 22 throughout this  adventure collecting all of these  objects that  meant something to her kind of like a  little kid would  my kids when i do their laundry the  stuff that they save throughout the day  and pick  up and it i just love that yeah so this  was a combination of trying to place  these  objects that 22 could collect during the  day and not make such a big deal out of  it the audience is like why is that  happening  that's going to be used later getting  this to work was so hard of course  because we also do that  transition where it starts with him  recalling 22's experiences  and then transition into him suddenly  remembering his own experiences and  those experiences he took for granted  when we were trying to create these  memories i remember we talked a lot  about  what are memories that would make us  feel warmth  inside that would make us feel loved and  comfortable and some of those memories  never changed  regb one of our story artists boarded  joe's feet in the sand  and i remember watching that going i can  feel that on my own feet  right at this moment  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  you

